
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last two things I need. First, scope — this determines how big the spec is.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether this deliverable is only the identity layer plus login, or also the lifecycle around it (logout,
  expired sessions) that the layer will immediately run into.

  - [1mIdentity module + login integration[0m — a small module owning read/write/clear of the userId, [38;5;153mlogin()[39m returning it,
    the submit handler storing it. Buys the seam your future forms consume, in the smallest reviewable piece. Costs a
    known gap: a stored userId can outlive its session cookie, so the app will believe someone is logged in when they
    aren't, until we handle it.
  - [1mAdd lifecycle: logout + stale-identity reconciliation[0m — plus an explicit clear, and a rule for what happens on load
    when the label exists but the session is gone. Buys an identity layer that's actually correct on day one. Costs more
    surface to design and approve now, some of it against API behavior we're assuming.
  - [1mAdd a second consumer to prove reuse[0m — plus wiring one more form to the layer. Buys evidence the interface is right
    before other code depends on it. Costs building a form you may not want yet; there's only a login form in [38;5;153mindex.html
[39m    today.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m the middle one. The stale-identity case isn't a future refinement — it's the first bug this design
  produces, and it's a security-shaped bug, since "app thinks you're logged in" is exactly the state you don't want
  wrong. Logout and clearing are also the cheapest to build while the module is being written and awkward to retrofit
  once forms depend on it. I'd skip the second consumer; the interface is small enough to get right without one.
